// Sample family: every person and record cited here is fictional.

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
William Henry Penhallow was the youngest child of #refp("john-penhallow") and #refp("elizabeth-trembath"). The family Bible gives his birth at Mineral Point on 14 February 1878.#src("william-birth-bible") He appears as a two-year-old in the 1880 census.#src("william-census-1880") In 1904 a county album described him as a miner in the zinc diggings near the town.#src("william-miner-1904")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Edith May Rowe gave her birthplace as Rowe's Diggings, both in the Bible and in the county marriage register.#src("edith-birth-bible")#src("edith-birth-marriage-register") No place of that name appears on any known map. An online family tree names her parents, but it cites no records, so this history does not name them.#src("edith-parents-tree")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
William and Edith married at Mineral Point on 12 June 1907, when he was twenty-nine and working as a miner and she was twenty-one.#src("penhallow-rowe-marriage-1907") That year Edith began the family register in the Bible, starting with their own wedding.#src("penhallow-rowe-marriage-bible")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Their daughter Lilian was born at Dodgeville in October 1908, and their son Harold in March 1910.#src("lilian-penhallow::lilian-birth-1908")#src("harold-penhallow::harold-birth-1910") Harold died on 24 October 1918, aged eight. Edith wrote in the Bible that he died of the influenza.#src("harold-penhallow::harold-death-1918")
]

// cite-lint: ok figure caption — says plainly that the photograph only illustrates the sample family
#fig("photographs/penhallow-family-group.jpg", caption: "Standing in for William and Edith with Lilian and Harold: an unidentified family photographed by the C.M. Bell studio, Library of Congress. Used to illustrate the sample family.")

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Their last child, #refp("robert-john-penhallow"), was born on 12 March 1921.#src("robert-john-penhallow::robert-birth-1921") By 1930 William owned a farm in Dodgeville township.#src("william-census-1930") The census that year lists him as a farmer, with Edith and their children Lilian, twenty-one, a schoolteacher, and Robert, nine.#src("william-farmer-1930")#src("lilian-penhallow::lilian-census-1930")#src("robert-john-penhallow::robert-census-1930")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
During the Second World War, Robert served in the army overseas, and Edith wrote to him from the farm. Her letter of Easter Sunday, 9 April 1944, tells him that his father had started plowing the low field, that Lilian was home from Madison, and that Joan Treloar had brought a pasty and asked after him.#src("edith-letter-1944")
]

// cite-lint: ok figure caption — identifies the facsimile and says it is fictional
#fig("letters/plates/1944-04-09-edith-penhallow-to-robert-plate.jpg", caption: "Edith Penhallow's letter to her son Robert, Easter Sunday, 9 April 1944. A facsimile made for the Flyleaf sample; the letter is fictional.")

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
Robert had written that he was near the sea in the old country, among people who talked the way his grandfather John used to. Edith wondered whether he was anywhere near St Just.#src("robert-john-penhallow::robert-letter-1944")
]

#block(breakable: false, below: 1.36em)[
William died at home on 18 January 1951.#src("william-death-1951") Edith died on 2 August 1964. Her own death is entered in the Bible in another hand.#src("edith-death-1964")
]
